{
  "title": "TKA Patellofemoral Alignment",
  "url": "https://www.orthobullets.com/recon/5017/tka-patellofemoral-alignment",
  "subtitle": "Updated: Jan 23 2025",
  "sections": [
    {
      "text": "Introduction",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "**Abnormal patellar tracking is the most common complication** of TKA.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "the most important variable in proper patellar tracking is preservation of a normal Q angle.",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Abnormal Q angle",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "an increase in the Q angle will lead to",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "an increased lateral subluxation forces on the patella relative to the trochlear groove",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "possible pain, mechanical symptoms, accelerated wear, and even dislocation.",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "it is critical to avoid techniques that lead to **increase Q angle**. Common errors include",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "**internal rotation of the femoral prosthesis**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "**medialization of the femoral component**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "**internal rotation or medialization of the tibial prosthesis**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "**placing the patellar prosthesis lateral on the patella**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Anatomy",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Q Angle",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "the Q angle is defined as angle between",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "axis of extensor mechanism (ASIS to center of patella)",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "axis of patellar tendon (center of patella to tibial tuberosity)",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Imaging",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "CT scan",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "malrotation of components is best diagnosed with CT scan of the knee",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Femoral Prosthesis",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "There are three reference axis that one may use:",
          "table": null,
          "images": [
            {
              "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/5017/images/epicondylar%20axis.jpg",
              "caption": "Shows axes of distal femur"
            }
          ],
          "importance": "5",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "**anteroposterior axis**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "defined as a line running from the center of the trochlear groove to the top of the intercondylar notch",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "-1",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "a line perpendicular to this defines the neutral rotational axis",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "-1",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "**transepicondylar axis**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "defined as a line running from the medial and lateral epicondyles the epicondylar axis is parallel to the cut tibial surface",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "-1",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "a posterior femoral cut parallel to the epicondylar axis will create the appropriate rectangular flexion gap",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "-1",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "**posterior condylar axis**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "defined as a line running across the tips of the two posterior condyles",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "-1",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "this line is in ~ 3 degrees of internal rotation from the transepicondylar axis, the femoral prosthesis should be externally rotated 3 degrees from this axis to produce a rectangular flexion gap",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "-1",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "if the lateral femoral condyle is hypoplastic, use of the posterior condylar axis may lead to internal rotation of the femoral component",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "-1",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "WARNING: the average posterior condylar twist angle is 3º but the range is 1-10º. Therefore vary angle of femoral rotation based on variances in femoral anatomy.",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "-1",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "**Internal Rotation of Femoral Prosthesis will Increase Q angle**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "4",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "by internally rotating the femoral prosthesis, you are effectively bringing the groove and the patella medially. This will increase the Q angle to the tibial tubercle",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "will also make the **medial compartment tight in flexion with subsequent TKA stiffness**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "**Medialization of the Femoral Prosthesis will Increase Q angle**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "a medialized femoral prosthesis will bring the trochlear groove to a more medial position, and thus bring the patella medial with it, thus increasing the Q angle",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "therefore, you want the femoral component to be slighly lateral if anything",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "2",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Tibial Prosthesis",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "The preferred rotation of the tibial component is neutral, with no internal or external rotation.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "the best way to obtain this is to have the tibial component centered over the medial third of the tibial tubercle",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "this may leave a portion of the posteromedial tibia uncovered and some overhang of the prosthesis over the tibia on the posterolateral tibia.",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "**Internal Rotation of Tibial Prosthesis will increase Q angle**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "internal rotation of the tibial component effectively results in relative external rotation of the tibial tubercle and an increase in the Q angle",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "2",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "**Medialization of tibia will increase Q angle**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "5",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Patellar Prosthesis",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "The preferred position of the patellar prosthesis is to be either centered over the patella or medialized",
          "table": null,
          "images": [
            {
              "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/5017/images/Patellar%20lateralization%20illustration%20small_moved.jpg",
              "caption": ""
            }
          ],
          "importance": "5",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "medializing the patellar component is one strategy to decrease the Q angle.",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "results in uncoverage of lateral facet. Consider removing to lessen risk of lateral facet syndrome.",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "another alternative is use of an oval shaped patella with the apex medialized.",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "**Lateralization of the patellar prosthesis will increase the Q angle and increase maltracking**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Intraoperative lateral subluxation of the patella",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "if patella laterally subluxes intraoperatively during trialing, deflate tourniquet and recheck before performing a lateral release",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Indications for resurfacing",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "absolute",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "inflammatory arthritis",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "patella maltracking",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "patellofemoral arthritis as the main indication for TKA",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Options for resurfacing during TKA",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "always resurface",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "never resurface",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "option to perform patelloplasty",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "excision of marginal osteophytes, reshaping of patella",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "3",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "selective resurfacing",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Patella resurfacing vs. Non-resurfacing",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "less anterior knee pain with resurfacing",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "less revision rates with resurfacing",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "inferior results with secondary resurfacing",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "similar patient satisfaction rates",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "trochlear design important: “patellar friendly”",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "thinner anterior flange",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "anatomic trochlear groove",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
